id: questions/what-am-i-to-be
title: "What am I to be?"
kind: orientation-question
status: preview
updated: 2026-09-12
epistemic_status: inferred
epistemic_note: >
  The prompt and its position are observed in yawn.bot source. The judgments
  below are inferred from that source and from an attributed handoff; each is
  a comparison with no proof, and `proof: none` is stated per judgment.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# This prompt is not new. It is the first of twelve authored entrance prompts
# on yawn.bot/new, defined in src/domain/yawn-entrance-questions.ts of
# yawn-ai/yawn.bot. This record holds its default judgments and the one thing
# about it the ordinary proof ladder gets wrong.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

surface:
  route: yawn.bot/new
  position: 1 of 12
  prompt_id: become
  authored_helper: "Describe what you want this Yawn to help you become, understand, or do."
  contract: interface/new-yawn-v0.1.yawn
  source_of_truth: src/domain/yawn-entrance-questions.ts

what_it_is_asking: >
  Not what this Yawn is about, and not why it matters — both of those are
  separate prompts two and three. This one asks what the person wants to
  become, understand, or do. Its answer is a commitment rather than a claim,
  which is what makes it different from every other prompt in the set.

answer_state:
  answer_status: unknown
  mapping_status: proposed
  rule: >
    The answer is source, not an accepted purpose. Silence is never inferred
    into an answer, and typing something here does not ratify it.

proof_kind:
  kind: self-binding
  contrast: world-falsifiable
  falsifier: "the person's own next move contradicts the stated to-be"
  verifier: "a run of moves consistent with it, with receipts"
  boundary: >
    No observation of the world refutes "I am to be a good dad." Only
    behaviour does. A self-binding answer must never be promoted by the
    ordinary proof ladder, because the ladder assumes reality can disconfirm
    the claim — so it would collect evidence that never touched it. Promote it
    only against behavioural evidence.

judgments:

  - id: become-before-subject
    chosen: a question about what to become, asked first
    rejected: naming the subject first
    differentiator: >
      someone arriving at /new has a pull, not a subject. naming the subject
      first forces a classification before there is anything to classify, and
      that classification then constrains every answer after it.
    proof: none

  - id: source-not-purpose
    chosen: the answer stays revisable source
    rejected: the first answer becomes the record's purpose
    differentiator: >
      a purpose that arrives by being typed first has no receipt. this is the
      prompt most easily mistaken for a commitment, so the separation between
      source and accepted purpose has to hold hardest here.
    proof: none

  - id: self-binding-over-falsifiable
    chosen: the next move falsifies a to-be answer
    rejected: the ordinary proof ladder
    differentiator: >
      no observation refutes "I am to be a good dad". only behaviour does.
      scored like an ordinary claim, an unfalsifiable sentence collects
      evidence that never touched it.
    proof: none

sibling_prompts:
  note: >
    The other eleven entrance prompts each carry their own default judgment,
    recorded in the same source file. The full set and its reasoning is in
    records/2026-09-12-bot-index-and-new-restructure.yawn.
  order: [become, identity, purpose, attention, direction, emotion, relationship, constraint, uncertainty, options, next, proof]

open_questions:
  - id: to-be-hard-gate
    question: >
      should an unanswered to-be gate objective ratification, or would that
      block every first move a person could make?
    status: unanswered
  - id: behavioural-verifier-source
    question: >
      what supplies the run of moves that verifies a to-be — the turn log, an
      explicit human review, or both?
    status: unanswered

boundary:
  - "A stated to-be is not authority."
  - "A stated to-be is not proof of character."
  - "Answering this prompt does not set an accepted purpose."
  - "A judgment here is a comparison, not a proof."
